<crash-analysis>
The crash is caused by a null pointer dereference in the `mg_connect_resolved` function. The `ifp` pointer is initialized from `c->mgr->ifp`, which is null in the fuzzing environment. The function then proceeds to use `ifp` without checking if it is null, leading to a crash.

A potential fix would be to add a null check for `ifp` at the beginning of the `mg_connect_resolved` function and handle the case where it is null gracefully, for example, by returning or setting an error state.

```c
void mg_connect_resolved(struct mg_connection *c) {
struct mg_tcpip_if *ifp = c->mgr->ifp;
if (ifp == NULL) {
// Handle the error, e.g., by closing the connection
// and logging an error message.
c->is_closing = 1;
return;
}
uint32_t rem_ip;
memcpy(&rem_ip, c->rem.ip, sizeof(uint32_t));
c->is_resolving = 0;
if (ifp->eport < MG_EPHEMERAL_PORT_BASE) ifp->eport = MG_EPHEMERAL_PORT_BASE;
memcpy(c->loc.ip, &ifp->ip, sizeof(uint32_t));
c->loc.port = mg_htons(ifp->eport++);
MG_DEBUG(("%lu %M -> %M", c->id, mg_print_ip_port, &c->loc, mg_print_ip_port,
&c->rem));
mg_call(c, MG_EV_RESOLVE, NULL);
c->is_connecting = 1;
if (c->is_udp && (rem_ip == 0xffffffff || rem_ip == (ifp->ip | ~ifp->mask))) {
struct connstate *s = (struct connstate *) (c + 1);
memset(s->mac, 0xFF, sizeof(s->mac));  // global or local broadcast
mac_resolved(c);
} else if (ifp->ip && ((rem_ip & ifp->mask) == (ifp->ip & ifp->mask)) &&
rem_ip != ifp->gw) {  // skip if gw (onstatechange -> READY -> ARP)
// If we're in the same LAN, fire an ARP lookup.
MG_DEBUG(("%lu ARP lookup...", c->id));
mg_tcpip_arp_request(ifp, rem_ip, NULL);
} else {
// Otherwise, send to gateway.
mg_tcpip_arp_request(ifp, ifp->gw, NULL);
}
}
```
</crash-analysis>

